    historyContent: {
      origins: "Napoleon is a classic English pub game first documented in H.E. Heather's October 1875 edition of The Bazaar. It quickly became England's national five-card game, displacing Loo. Early authors described it as 'spirited and interesting', requiring 'considerable judgement and skill'. The expression 'going Nap' (meaning to risk all on one attempt) derives from this game.",
      originCountries: [
        'UK',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1875: Earliest rules published by H.E. Heather in The Bazaar',
        '1885: American Hoyle incorrectly claims French origin; more likely European roots with north German Fipsen as a relative',
        '1891: Bids of Wellington, BlÃ¼cher and MisÃ¨re added; variants like Peep Nap, Sir Garnet, and Jam emerge',
        "1982: Andrew Pennycook notes Nap was 'widely played in pubs'",
        'Present: Still played in southern England, Strathclyde (Scotland), and other parts of Britain',
      ],
      evolution: 'The game evolved from simple five-card bidding to include advanced bids like Wellington (overcalls Nap) and BlÃ¼cher (overcalls Wellington). Variants emerged including Peep Nap (with floater card), Purchase Nap (buying cards), and six-, seven-, and nine-card versions.',
      cultural: 'A quintessential British pub game played for small stakes with settlement after each hand. The terminology reflects British military history, with bids named after the Duke of Wellington and General BlÃ¼cher who defeated Napoleon at Waterloo.',
    },
